The idea: CAM-UV builds on CAM (Causal Additive Model, Bühlmann et al. 2014). It extends it to handle unobserved common causes (hidden confounders, which they call "UV" - unobserved variables). It tests for the presence of unobserved common causes and can identify the causal graph among observed variables even in the presence of such confounders.

Main components of CAM-UV:
1. Fitting a CAM to observed data — regress each observed variable on others in an additive (GAM) fashion.
2. Residuals analysis: If there is no unobserved confounder between X and Y, the residuals of the additive regression should be independent of the "other" variables. The method uses the concept that model misspecification due to hidden common causes manifests in certain residual correlations.
3. It essentially: (a) finds the causal order/permutation among observed variables, and (b) identifies a set of "candidate unobserved common causes" (UVC - unobserved variable candidates). 

Actually let me recall the exact procedure from the paper "CAM-UV: A general, robust and scalable method for causal discovery with hidden common causes" (Schultheiss, Bühlmann, 2023, arXiv:2303.00448?).

Key steps:
1. Given observed data X_1,...,X_d, fit a CAM: for each variable j, regress it on all others to get residuals.
2. Use independence tests on residuals to construct a graph of "conditional independencies" — this is similar to the DAG structure learning for CAM but adapted.
3. The method outputs: a directed causal graph among observed variables where some edges may be labeled as having an underlying hidden common cause (i.e., the edge might be spurious due to a hidden confounder).

The core insight: under a CAM with hidden common causes, one can regress each observed variable on subsets and detect whether a pair of variables shares a hidden common cause by testing whether the residual of one is independent of the other.

The CAM-UV algorithm specifically (from the paper) has two main procedures:
- First, it learns the causal order among a set of "strongly connected components" (SCCs) of an undirected skeleton, and 
- Then within/between SCCs it identifies candidate hidden common causes.

